# Every exposure that had nowhere to go. One row per layer where every buffer is accounted for and
# none of them has room, beside the exposure its own two measurements imply.
#
# The argument is the whole layer
#   The argument is the whole layer and never one buffer. Read as "the capacity buffer is a stated
#   zero", this is one route closed rather than nowhere to go: the same layer could still have drawn
#   on stock or made the demand wait. Two rules rest on this population, and from that premise both
#   would conclude that somebody went without, which it does not support. The three buffers stand in
#   for one another, and the buffer belongs in a column, which is where assets/ddl/schema.ddl keeps
#   it.
#
# One unmeasured buffer suspends it
#   A buffer nobody measured licenses none of this, because nobody knows whether the supply could
#   have stretched that way. That holds of capacity, of inventory and of time alike.
#
# It rests on `stated`, not on `measured`
#   Every zero carries a `boundOrigin`, so the population can be read back, and none of the capacity
#   zeros was measured: they are `contractual`, a reserved block somebody negotiated, and
#   `intrinsic`, a material limit. A shelf life entails a zero, a negotiation fixes one, an
#   observation records one, and each is somebody saying the buffer is empty and standing behind it.
#
# Two rules, two questions
#   Two rules read this relation, and they ask different questions of it. One wants the layers with
#   no named holder; the other wants every one of them, against the shares that were admitted.
#   Written out at both call sites, these joins would be two copies of one population, one edit
#   away from two populations under one argument.
-- layers/exposure_scope.sqlc, restricted to the standing that licenses a conclusion.
SELECT s.*
FROM (
    :compose(layers/exposure_scope.sqlc)
) s
WHERE s.standing = 'every buffer sized and empty'
